WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.892
Upload paths and large request bodies need explicit limits.

2
00:00:03.892 --> 00:00:08.378
Body size limits may be global, endpoint-specific, or host-specific.

3
00:00:08.378 --> 00:00:21.044
A public profile image upload, an internal import endpoint, a document submission workflow, and a machine-to-machine data feed should not automatically share the same request body assumptions.

4
00:00:21.044 --> 00:00:25.331
Large bodies can consume memory, disk, CPU, and backend capacity.

5
00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:32.192
Buffering, temporary storage, scanning, parsing, transformation, and downstream processing all add cost.

6
00:00:32.192 --> 00:00:38.921
Even when the server eventually rejects a request, the path to that rejection can still use resources.

7
00:00:38.921 --> 00:00:42.945
Controls should be placed early enough to protect the system.

8
00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:46.111
Request decompression deserves defensive review.

9
00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:50.729
Compressed bodies may be smaller on the wire than after decompression.

10
00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:56.732
The application should still constrain decompressed size through endpoint or server limits.

11
00:00:56.732 --> 00:01:06.693
The risk is not only storage size; it is also CPU time, memory pressure, parser behavior, and the downstream systems that receive the expanded content.

12
00:01:06.693 --> 00:01:12.564
Decompression-bomb risk should be handled conceptually as a resource-consumption concern.

13
00:01:12.564 --> 00:01:21.602
The defensive response is to define size limits, timeouts, safe decompression behavior, endpoint scope, monitoring, and failure handling.

14
00:01:21.602 --> 00:01:29.452
The course does not need construction details to teach the engineering lesson: expanded content still needs boundaries.

15
00:01:29.452 --> 00:01:42.711
File upload controls should include size, type, storage location, generated server-side names, scanning or review workflow where appropriate, retention rules, authorization, and business justification.

16
00:01:42.711 --> 00:01:50.957
Uploaded files should be processed by a documented workflow, not by a generic endpoint that accepts anything for convenience.

17
00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:55.311
The practical baseline is to make large-request behavior explicit.

18
00:01:55.311 --> 00:02:08.372
Know which endpoints accept large bodies, why they need them, where data is buffered, which limits apply, how failures are reported, and which logs or metrics tell operators when limits are reached.
